<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Northumberland</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Northumberland is the northernmost county of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>, between the Tyne and the Tweed, crossed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hadrians-wall">Hadrian's Wall</a>. Formerly the northern part of the Anglian kingdom of Northumbria, it was long disputed with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a>: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/david">David</a> I's son Henry held the earldom from 1139, until Henry II reclaimed it in 1157. Its border lands remained a zone of raids and fortified towers into the sixteenth century.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/northumberland.jpg" alt="Northumberland" width="320" height="158" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: mattbuck · CC BY-SA 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bamburgh_MMB_55_Bamburgh_Castle_(edited,_cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Northumberland is low and flat near the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-sea">North Sea</a> coast and becomes increasingly mountainous towards the north-west. The west contains part of the Cheviot Hills and the North Pennines, and the Cheviot, at 815 metres, is the highest point. The Cheviot Hills consist mainly of Devonian granite and andesite lava, and the Whin Sill, an intrusion of dolerite on which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hadrians-wall">Hadrian's Wall</a> runs, forms a second ridge. The North Tyne and South Tyne join near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hexham">Hexham</a>, and the Tweed forms part of the Scottish border. Because of its latitude and high ground it is one of the coldest areas of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>, though rainfall is relatively low.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The name is recorded in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anglo-saxon-chronicle">Anglo-Saxon Chronicle</a> as norð hẏmbra land, the land north of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/humber">Humber</a>, and the kingdom of Northumbria took its name from the Old English for the people or province north of the Humber. The county has a rich prehistory, with rock art, hillforts such as Yeavering Bell and stone circles such as the Goatstones and Duddo Five Stones. Most of the area was occupied by the Brythonic Celtic Votadini, with the Brigantes to the south. In the Roman era most of the county lay north of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hadrians-wall">Hadrian's Wall</a>.</p><p>Much of the county's history was defined by its position on a border. The region was contested between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a> into the early modern era, which led to the building of many castles, peel towers and bastle houses and to the early modern fortifications at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berwick-upon-tweed">Berwick-upon-Tweed</a>. The earldom was held by the Scottish royal family by marriage between 1139 and 1157 and again between 1215 and 1217, and Scotland gave up all claims to the region in the Treaty of York of 1237. As lords in the Scottish Marches, the earls of Northumberland held the task of protecting England from Scottish retaliation for English invasions, and wielded significant power in English affairs.</p><p>Northumberland is also associated with Celtic Christianity, particularly the tidal island of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lindisfarne">Lindisfarne</a>. Coal mining in the county goes back to Tudor times, and during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/industrial-revolution">Industrial Revolution</a> the area had significant coal mining, shipbuilding and armaments industries.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Northumberland is the northernmost county of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>, a ceremonial county in the North East bordering <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a>. It is largely rural, and its administrative centre is Morpeth. It contains Northumberland National Park and the Farne Islands, known for their bird life. It has its own traditions, among them the rapper sword dance, the clog dance and the Northumbrian smallpipe, and its own tartan.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Northumberland” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Great Domesday covers most of the rest of England and parts of Wales, but not the northern lands that later became Westmorland, Cumberland, <b>Northumberland</b> and the County Palatine of Durham.</blockquote><a href="/work/domesday-book">Domesday Book · What It Is</a></li><li><blockquote>Days after Edward VI's death, the Duke of <b>Northumberland</b> had his Protestant daughter-in-law proclaimed queen to bar the Catholic Mary Tudor.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/july-10">10 July in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Malcolm III was killed in an ambush while besieging Alnwick Castle in <b>Northumberland</b>, together with his eldest son.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/november-13">13 November in history</a></li><li><blockquote>HMS <b>Northumberland</b> anchored off Jamestown, Saint Helena, carrying the defeated Napoleon.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/october-15">15 October in history</a></li><li><blockquote>At York, Henry III of England and Alexander II of Scotland agreed that <b>Northumberland</b>, Cumberland and Westmorland were subject to English sovereignty.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/september-25">25 September in history</a></li></ul></section>
